#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173220 :tutorial/content "We know that if a random variable $X$ follows a *discrete* uniform distribution, then it has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that\n\n$$\nX\\sim U\\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are integers. In other words, we're concerned with the special case where the support $S$ of $X$ contains every integer between $a$ and $b$ inclusive.\n\nIn this case, it can be shown that the mean value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\left.\\dfrac{\\mid S\\right|^2-1}{12}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "8f4d7b80-6d8f-5618-b9a4-a8041941041e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6456 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173221 :tutorial/content "Here, we will show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nBy the definition of expected value for discrete random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S)xf(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x ∈ S)x \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\begin{vmatrix}} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid}∑_(x ∈ S)x \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}}[a + (a + 1) + (a + 2) + ⋯ + b]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the term in the square brackets is an arithmetic series with the first term $a$ and the last term $b$. Therefore, using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\begin{vmatrix}}[a + (a + 1) + (a + 2) + ⋯ + b] \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &} \\cdot \\frac{\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}}{2} \\cdot (a + b) \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &} \\cdot \\frac{\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}}{2} \\cdot (a + b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{a + b}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee8590ba-00d0-5dce-a5c6-f69f23dd1dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6458 :tutorial/title "Proving the Formula for the Mean"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173222 :tutorial/content "Let's now show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\left.\\dfrac{\\mid S\\right|^2-1}{12}\n$$\n\nWe will assume, without loss of generality, that the distribution of our random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, \\mid S \\mid \\}\n$$\n\nWe do not lose generality here because, in general, the variance (i.e., spread) of a random variable is unaffected by translations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X + c] = \\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nBy the definition of $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S)x^{2}f(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x ∈ S)x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\begin{vmatrix}} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid} \\cdot ∑_(x ∈ S)x^{2} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} + 2^{2} + ⋯ + & S & ^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n i^2 = \\dfrac16 n (n+1)(2n+1)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} + 2^{2} + ⋯ + & S & ^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot & S\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2 \\mid S \\mid + 1) \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot & S\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2 \\mid S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2 \\mid S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1))/(6)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2, \\qquad \\textrm {E}[X] = \\dfrac{1+ \\mid S \\mid}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - (E[X])^{2} \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(6) - (\\frac{\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(6) - ((\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1)^{2})/(4) \\\\\n&= (2(\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12) - (3(\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1)^{2})/(12) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12)(2(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1) - 3(\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1)) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12)(4\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 2 - 3\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}- 3) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12)(\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}- 1) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}- 1))/(12) \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{\\mid S\\right|^{2} - 1}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "3ac5d5b0-adb4-5096-93dc-1a400ec42c5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6459 :tutorial/title "Proving the Formula for the Variance"}]}
